A||N||5||kornholio||headlight motor gone?||||1||
Z||000000||kornholio||11-20-2000||10:15 PM||||I turned on my lights today and the right one only came up half way, so I lifted the headlight door and it still did not raise all the way.  I then opened the hood and turned the lights on and off.  The light would only go up half way and the manual turn knob kept turning.  I then pulled the light all the way up and the knob stopped turning.  With the hood lowered, I tested the lights a couple times and they worked perfectly fine.<BR>So my question is:  What just happened here???  I'm pretty sure that there was no ice or snow keeping it from opening all the way.  Thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000001||sqoach||11-20-2000||10:24 PM||||hmm...i'm guessin your plastic gear inside your headlight motor is starting to wear.  that happened to my passenger side one, but it never worked after that so i had to buy a new motor.  i'm guessing yours just happened to start catching again.  if the gear does finally wear out, i think you can buy another from rodney dickman's site.<P><P>------------------<BR>sQoaCh||||reg||1
Z||000002||2xsess||11-20-2000||11:27 PM||||I think sqoach is correct, sounds like the gear is going bad. You can get the gear set from Rodney, $45 for a set. I just did one of mine last week, works great  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/My_car_(large)sig_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||GarethWright||11-21-2000||06:17 PM||||On the 87-88 system, there is a bushing that wears out(translation, gets destroyed) in the headlight motor. You<BR>                          can get a rebuild kit with the bushings from Ecklers Corvette (mail order place out of Titusville, FL. Phone number:<BR>                          1-800-327-4868) for about $5. The part number is E-29304. The Corvette headlight motors use the same bushing.<BR>                          If you take the gear end of the motor apart (don't take the Motor end completely apart, as the spring-loaded<BR>                          contacts are a pain to get back in) Inside there's large white plastic gear, with a metal cap that is attached to the<BR>                          arm that pushes the light assembly up.<BR>                          If you take the metal cap off of the plastic gear, likely you fill find lots of little plastic crumbs. Those are the<BR>                          remains of the 3 round bushings, Like 3 "O"s. When the motor strains too hard on something, instead of stripping<BR>                          the large white gear, it just smashes those little round bushings. Its quite easy to do, it took me about 15 mins<BR>                          (after gettin the motor end back together  ) Hope that was Some help to Somebody <BR>                          Gareth<P>                          Oh yea, mine worked fine with the hood open, the bushing crumbs give enough grip to lift the headlight as long as<BR>                          theres no resistance (aka spring loaded headlight door)||||reg||1
Z||000004||Tom88gt||11-21-2000||08:59 PM||||rodney's kit!!!  fixed mine!||||reg||14
Z||000005||Rodney||11-21-2000||10:57 PM||||I have the gaskets for the 87-88 headlight motors in the works. The side cover gasket and the motor gasket. Maybe in 4-5 weeks? I may just include the side cover gaskets with all the rebuild kits but the price will be slightly more. Or maybe a deluxe kit with some silicone grease, o-ring and the gasket? ||||reg||1
